  "client_view_text": " Below is a write-ahead log (WAL) recovery procedure. Recovering from a Crash. A write-ahead log (WAL) is a feature of some databases that stores all the write operations before they are committed to the database. During recovery, the database reads all the transactions from the WAL, checks their validity and rolls back if necessary. The database will then commit the transactions that are valid and consistent. The goal is to have a consistent view of the database at the end of the recovery process. The database can then be brought back online and used by the application. This process relies on the database being able to read the transactions from It works as follows: the **journal** is stored in memory (RAM), and is a sorted list of all operations that need to be written to the database. When the database crashes, the journal is also lost. However, the database knows which operation was in progress, so it can recover by rolling back the transactions in the journal that were started before the crash (i.e., those that were in the journal and not yet written to the database), and then re-applying the transactions that were in progress when the database crashed. The database can do this by scanning the journal, identifying which transactions were in progress when it crashed, and replaying those. To implement this, the database needs to use a data structure to keep track of which transactions are in progress. How could it do that?\n\n## Step 1: Understand the concept of a write-ahead log\nA write-ahead log, also known as a journal, is a data structure used by a database to maintain a record of all operations (e.g., insert, update, delete) that need to be written to the database. This log is stored in memory (RAM) for efficiency.\n\n## Step 2: Recognize the key components involved in recovery\nAfter a crash, the database needs to recover by rolling"
